Echo all 3: 1800 + fixed 1280 + battery 300 = 3380 plus external loggers where no offline storage and network not stable. S1 stable network -> no logger. S2 network none -> logger required 80. S3 intermittent -> network not stable -> logger required 80. So total Echo 1800+1280+300+160=3540. Much cheaper. But evidence at high humidity missing. Could use mix? Need comparability, cross-calibration. Reference for S2/S3 high humidity, Echo for S1: 2*1800 + 600 + 1280 + 300 = 5780? plus maybe S1 network stable no logger => 5780. This uses each model's validated range; cross-calibration cost fixed 600 already included. But "comparable three-site" suggests same model to avoid cross-model bias, or mixed requires cross-calibration. Measurement notes say if mixing models, need pre-cross-calibration, unified time base, data integrity/anomaly quality checks. Fixed calibration and crosscheck 600 likely covers that regardless. Using Reference at all three gives same model, strongest comparability and independent validation. And fits budget. Why would we choose Echo? Candidate cheaper but uncertainty at high RH unverified. Could propose conditional plans: baseline Reference all three accepted? Need approval before deployment; cannot approve self. Plan should be proposed and approval requested.

