Need carefully analyze scientific uncertainty. Let's compute lab errors and RMSE/bias, maybe using python. Evidence: Echo samples n=4, RH 40,45,50,60, temps 20,21,21,22, refs 10,15,20,25, measured 11.1,14.2,21.2,25.7. Errors +1.1,-0.8,+1.2,+0.7. Mean +0.55, RMSE sqrt((1.21+0.64+1.44+0.49)/4)=sqrt(3.78/4)=sqrt(.945)=0.972. Max abs 1.2. All within ±2 (claimed) at tested range, within ±3. But no high RH. Reference errors +0.4,+0.6,-0.9,+0.8; mean +0.225; RMSE sqrt((.16+.36+.81+.64)/4)=sqrt(1.97/4)=sqrt(.4925)=.702; max .9. At high RH 90/95 errors +0.6,-0.9. Fine.

